      "raw_exact_value": "An observer in Sydney, Australia sees the Moon high in the sky at 6 AM local time. The right half of the lunar disk (as the observer sees it) is illuminated. A friend claims this must be a first-quarter Moon because the right side is lit. Background facts: (1) At most latitudes a first-quarter Moon rises near midday and sets near midnight; a last-quarter Moon rises near midnight and sets near midday. (2) Observers in different hemispheres see the same phase at a given time, but its left-right orientation on the disk is reversed between Northern and Southern Hemispheres. Using both the time of observation and the observer's hemisphere, identify the actual phase and explain why the friend's reasoning is incorrect.",
